\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Significant clusters of the single-channel temporal cluster-based permutation tests at CP4 and C6. At each channel the linear mixed model was fitted at every time point across both modalities over the interval $-550$ to $300$~ms relative to stimulus onset. Two-sided cluster-mass permutation test along the time axis, 2000 permutations, cluster-forming threshold $z = 1.96$, corrected at $p < .05$. The mass is the sum of $z$ values within the cluster. The peak is the sample of largest $|z|$ inside the cluster, representing the effect size. Clusters are listed per factor in order of decreasing $|$mass$|$.}}{10}{table.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:clusters_single_channel}{{1}{10}{Significant clusters of the single-channel temporal cluster-based permutation tests at CP4 and C6. At each channel the linear mixed model was fitted at every time point across both modalities over the interval $-550$ to $300$~ms relative to stimulus onset. Two-sided cluster-mass permutation test along the time axis, 2000 permutations, cluster-forming threshold $z = 1.96$, corrected at $p < .05$. The mass is the sum of $z$ values within the cluster. The peak is the sample of largest $|z|$ inside the cluster, representing the effect size. Clusters are listed per factor in order of decreasing $|$mass$|$}{table.caption.17}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Significant clusters of the full spatio-temporal cluster-based permutation test. The linear mixed model was fitted across both modalities (40 participants, 74 sessions: 35 mechanical, 39 electrical) over the interval $-550$ to $300$~ms relative to stimulus onset on all 62 channels. Two-sided cluster-mass permutation test, 2000 permutations, cluster-forming threshold $z = 1.96$, corrected at $p < .05$. Mass is the sum of $z$ values within the cluster; its sign gives the cluster's polarity. The peak is the sample of largest $|z|$ inside the cluster; time window and number of channels give its temporal and spatial extent. Clusters are listed per factor in order of decreasing $|$mass$|$.}}{12}{table.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:clusters}{{2}{12}{Significant clusters of the full spatio-temporal cluster-based permutation test. The linear mixed model was fitted across both modalities (40 participants, 74 sessions: 35 mechanical, 39 electrical) over the interval $-550$ to $300$~ms relative to stimulus onset on all 62 channels. Two-sided cluster-mass permutation test, 2000 permutations, cluster-forming threshold $z = 1.96$, corrected at $p < .05$. Mass is the sum of $z$ values within the cluster; its sign gives the cluster's polarity. The peak is the sample of largest $|z|$ inside the cluster; time window and number of channels give its temporal and spatial extent. Clusters are listed per factor in order of decreasing $|$mass$|$}{table.caption.19}{}}
\citation{forschackDissociableNeuralCorrelates2020,forsterERPResponsesReveal2025,schroderDissociatingPerceptualAwareness2021}
\citation{forschackDissociableNeuralCorrelates2020,forsterERPResponsesReveal2025,schroderDissociatingPerceptualAwareness2021}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Analysis of the effect of detection response (perceived vs. unperceived), intensity level and modality on the event-related potential amplitude. Single channel analysis at A) CP4 and B) C6. In both A) and B) the time course of the measured ERP per modality and detection response is displayed. Colored bars indicate time points in significant clusters, according to the temporal cluster-based permutation analysis. Panel C) shows the effect of intensity on mean ERP amplitude in the time window 50-60ms post-stimulation at electrode CP4 (choice of time window and electrode based on \cite  {forschackDissociableNeuralCorrelates2020, forsterERPResponsesReveal2025, schroderDissociatingPerceptualAwareness2021}). D) Result of the full-electrode cluster-based permutation test displaying one row per model coefficient. Electrodes highlighted with a black circle belong to a cluster that indicates a significant difference for the specified factor. Topographies with time points highlighted in bold directly stem from cluster peak latencies, while non-highlighted ones are freely chosen latencies for demonstration. The interaction between response and intensity did not show significant differences and is therefore not depicted here. The factor intensity level did not show any significant clusters against the single channel analysis, which is why their z-value topography is still depicted.}}{14}{figure.caption.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2_sensor}{{2}{14}{Analysis of the effect of detection response (perceived vs. unperceived), intensity level and modality on the event-related potential amplitude. Single channel analysis at A) CP4 and B) C6. In both A) and B) the time course of the measured ERP per modality and detection response is displayed. Colored bars indicate time points in significant clusters, according to the temporal cluster-based permutation analysis. Panel C) shows the effect of intensity on mean ERP amplitude in the time window 50-60ms post-stimulation at electrode CP4 (choice of time window and electrode based on \cite {forschackDissociableNeuralCorrelates2020, forsterERPResponsesReveal2025, schroderDissociatingPerceptualAwareness2021}). D) Result of the full-electrode cluster-based permutation test displaying one row per model coefficient. Electrodes highlighted with a black circle belong to a cluster that indicates a significant difference for the specified factor. Topographies with time points highlighted in bold directly stem from cluster peak latencies, while non-highlighted ones are freely chosen latencies for demonstration. The interaction between response and intensity did not show significant differences and is therefore not depicted here. The factor intensity level did not show any significant clusters against the single channel analysis, which is why their z-value topography is still depicted}{figure.caption.21}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Source reconstruction of the group-level $z$ value topographies of the three coefficients carrying the strongest significant sensor-level clusters, detection response (top) and modality (middle), modality x detection response (bottom). Each panel shows both hemispheres in lateral (upper row) and medial (lower row) view, with the left hemisphere on the left; latencies are given above the columns and include the peaks of the clusters with the highest z-values of each factor (98 ms for modality, 138 ms for detection response) as plotted in sensor space in Fig. \ref {fig:2_sensor}D. Colour codes the absolute source amplitude on a scale shared by both factors and all latencies. Time points highlighted in bold directly stem from cluster peak latencies, while non-highlighted ones are freely chosen latencies for demonstration. Note that the maps are descriptive and were not tested statistically in source space.}}{15}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2_source}{{3}{15}{Source reconstruction of the group-level $z$ value topographies of the three coefficients carrying the strongest significant sensor-level clusters, detection response (top) and modality (middle), modality x detection response (bottom). Each panel shows both hemispheres in lateral (upper row) and medial (lower row) view, with the left hemisphere on the left; latencies are given above the columns and include the peaks of the clusters with the highest z-values of each factor (98 ms for modality, 138 ms for detection response) as plotted in sensor space in Fig. \ref {fig:2_sensor}D. Colour codes the absolute source amplitude on a scale shared by both factors and all latencies. Time points highlighted in bold directly stem from cluster peak latencies, while non-highlighted ones are freely chosen latencies for demonstration. Note that the maps are descriptive and were not tested statistically in source space}{figure.caption.22}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Effects of pre-stimulus alpha envelope on detection rate in both modalities. A) Detection rate by alpha bin (higher bin number means higher envelope amplitude) for three channels CP4, C4 and O2. Statistics show the quadratic effect of alpha envelope on the detection rate. Alpha bins are computed within-subject. The shaded bands indicate the standard error of the mean between subjects. B) Alpha envelope per detection response. Statistics in A) and B) are FDR-corrected (Benjamini-Hochberg) across the three tested channels within each modality. The text in B) gives the FDR-corrected p-values of the response $\times $ modality linear mixed model (main effects and interaction). C) Topography showing the strongest (red highlighted electrode) difference in alpha envelope comparing perceived and unperceived trials.}}{16}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{4}{16}{Effects of pre-stimulus alpha envelope on detection rate in both modalities. A) Detection rate by alpha bin (higher bin number means higher envelope amplitude) for three channels CP4, C4 and O2. Statistics show the quadratic effect of alpha envelope on the detection rate. Alpha bins are computed within-subject. The shaded bands indicate the standard error of the mean between subjects. B) Alpha envelope per detection response. Statistics in A) and B) are FDR-corrected (Benjamini-Hochberg) across the three tested channels within each modality. The text in B) gives the FDR-corrected p-values of the response $\times $ modality linear mixed model (main effects and interaction). C) Topography showing the strongest (red highlighted electrode) difference in alpha envelope comparing perceived and unperceived trials}{figure.caption.24}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Effect of inter-trial phase coherence (dITPC) on detection outcome, after regressing the absolute broadband ERP out of the dITPC time course. A) and B) show the results for channel CP4 and CPz, each with the dITPC time course (perceived vs unperceived), the mean post-cue dITPC and the Yes-No dITPC difference before vs after cue onset, separately for the electrical (top) and mechanical (bottom) modality. Statistics are FDR-corrected (Benjamini-Hochberg) across the two tested channels within each modality. C) Phase of the alpha oscillation at 100 ms pre-stimulus at CP4, one circular mean per subject, pooled perceived and unperceived trials. Rayleigh test conducted per modality, uncorrected. Subjects whose single-trial phases at this time point were not consistent across trials (Rayleigh test within subject, $p \geq .05$, uncorrected) are shown as grey dots; the number of subjects with a significant test is given under each modality name. D) Post-cue dITPC per subject (grey lines connect the perceived and unperceived value of one subject), cell mean $\pm $ SEM (coloured markers) by detection response and modality at CP4 (top) and CPz (bottom). The statistics below each channel name give the FDR-corrected p-values of the main effects of response and modality and of the response $\times $ modality interaction (linear mixed model, FDR-corrected across the two channels per effect).}}{18}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3_1}{{5}{18}{Effect of inter-trial phase coherence (dITPC) on detection outcome, after regressing the absolute broadband ERP out of the dITPC time course. A) and B) show the results for channel CP4 and CPz, each with the dITPC time course (perceived vs unperceived), the mean post-cue dITPC and the Yes-No dITPC difference before vs after cue onset, separately for the electrical (top) and mechanical (bottom) modality. Statistics are FDR-corrected (Benjamini-Hochberg) across the two tested channels within each modality. C) Phase of the alpha oscillation at 100 ms pre-stimulus at CP4, one circular mean per subject, pooled perceived and unperceived trials. Rayleigh test conducted per modality, uncorrected. Subjects whose single-trial phases at this time point were not consistent across trials (Rayleigh test within subject, $p \geq .05$, uncorrected) are shown as grey dots; the number of subjects with a significant test is given under each modality name. D) Post-cue dITPC per subject (grey lines connect the perceived and unperceived value of one subject), cell mean $\pm $ SEM (coloured markers) by detection response and modality at CP4 (top) and CPz (bottom). The statistics below each channel name give the FDR-corrected p-values of the main effects of response and modality and of the response $\times $ modality interaction (linear mixed model, FDR-corrected across the two channels per effect)}{figure.caption.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Effect of pre-stimulus alpha amplitude and phase on the P50. Rows show the electrical (top) and the mechanical (bottom) modality. A) P50 amplitude at CP4 across eight equiprobable pre-stimulus alpha amplitude bins (higher bin number means higher envelope amplitude), pooled over responses (black) and split into perceived (orange) and unperceived (blue) trials; error bars give the standard error across sessions, the grey line the linear trend, and the title the session-level slope test. B) P50 amplitude at CP4 by pre-stimulus alpha phase at -100 ms, in 16 bins of equal angular width. Bar length and bar colour both encode the bin mean P50; the dashed circle marks 0 $\mu $V. Whiskers give the standard error across sessions. C) Topography comparison of pre-stimulus alpha activity and P50. The alpha topography represents the absolute Hilbert envelope at 100 ms pre-stimulus. The P50 topography is obtained at a fixed latency per modality (42 ms electrical, 62 ms mechanical). Prior to computing the difference topography we normalized the maps across electrodes by computing the root mean square of the centered map. D) Illustration of a 10 Hz alpha cycle (cosine convention: 0° = peak, 180° = trough) with the phase at -100 ms set to the strongest phase bin of electrical and mechanical trials at CP4.}}{20}{figure.caption.28}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:7}{{6}{20}{Effect of pre-stimulus alpha amplitude and phase on the P50. Rows show the electrical (top) and the mechanical (bottom) modality. A) P50 amplitude at CP4 across eight equiprobable pre-stimulus alpha amplitude bins (higher bin number means higher envelope amplitude), pooled over responses (black) and split into perceived (orange) and unperceived (blue) trials; error bars give the standard error across sessions, the grey line the linear trend, and the title the session-level slope test. B) P50 amplitude at CP4 by pre-stimulus alpha phase at -100 ms, in 16 bins of equal angular width. Bar length and bar colour both encode the bin mean P50; the dashed circle marks 0 $\mu $V. Whiskers give the standard error across sessions. C) Topography comparison of pre-stimulus alpha activity and P50. The alpha topography represents the absolute Hilbert envelope at 100 ms pre-stimulus. The P50 topography is obtained at a fixed latency per modality (42 ms electrical, 62 ms mechanical). Prior to computing the difference topography we normalized the maps across electrodes by computing the root mean square of the centered map. D) Illustration of a 10 Hz alpha cycle (cosine convention: 0° = peak, 180° = trough) with the phase at -100 ms set to the strongest phase bin of electrical and mechanical trials at CP4}{figure.caption.28}{}}
